WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.400
Welcome back. Clog code hooks let you run your own commands automatically at specific moments,

2
00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:12.800
and there are quite a few of them. The trick is knowing when events fire and which ones let you

3
00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:19.280
step in versus just watch. This lesson maps the events by cadence and shows which hooks block

4
00:00:19.280 --> 00:00:25.280
and which only observe. Let's get into it. A hook is just a command you register to run

5
00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:30.399
automatically when a specific lifecycle event fires. You aren't changing the model,

6
00:00:30.399 --> 00:00:36.560
you are attaching your own script to a moment. A lifecycle event is that named moment. It might be

7
00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:43.439
a tool call about to happen, a session starting, or a turn ending, and each one gives your hooks

8
00:00:43.439 --> 00:00:50.159
a chance to run. A blocking hook is one that can actually stop the action from going through,

9
00:00:50.159 --> 00:00:56.000
and the way it signals that is by exiting with code 2. Everything else in this lesson is just

10
00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:01.520
events at different moments, and whether the hook at that moment can block or only observe.

11
00:01:02.240 --> 00:01:08.480
That single distinction is the whole game. The cleanest way to hold all these events in your

12
00:01:08.480 --> 00:01:14.400
head is to group them by cadence, by how often they fire. There are three buckets. The outermost

13
00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:20.800
is once per session, session start, and session end fire, one time each, framing the whole session

14
00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:26.559
like bookends. The middle bucket is once per turn, prompt submit and stop fire around each

15
00:01:26.559 --> 00:01:32.400
individual thing you ask the agent to do, and the innermost, fastest bucket, is every tool call.

16
00:01:32.959 --> 00:01:38.800
Pre-tool use and post-tool use wrap each and every tool the agent runs inside its loop,

17
00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:45.440
so those can fire many times in a single turn. Session, turn, tool. Outer to inner,

18
00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:50.879
slow to fast. If you remember that nesting, the events you will reach for most fall into place.

19
00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:57.839
Let's start at the outermost layer, the session events. These fire once as a session opens and

20
00:01:57.839 --> 00:02:04.400
once as it closes. Session start runs when a session begins or resumes, so it is your hook

21
00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:09.679
for the very beginning. Session end runs when the session terminates, right at shutdown.

22
00:02:10.240 --> 00:02:15.600
Here is the key property. Both of these are observe only. They can't block anything,

23
00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:20.800
because there is no single action for them to veto. They simply mark the edges, so what are

24
00:02:20.800 --> 00:02:26.720
they good for? Session start is where you set up context as you enter, maybe loading project notes

25
00:02:26.720 --> 00:02:33.119
or environment details. Session end is where you clean things up on the way out. Think of them as

26
00:02:33.119 --> 00:02:40.240
setup and teardown, not as gates. One layer in, we hit the turn events, and these bracket each turn

27
00:02:40.240 --> 00:02:46.479
you take with the agent. User prompt submit fires when you send a prompt, before Claude even reads

28
00:02:46.479 --> 00:02:52.080
it. Stop fires on the other side when Claude finishes responding. Now here is what makes

29
00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:58.080
these interesting. Because prompt submit runs before your prompt is processed, a hook there

30
00:02:58.080 --> 00:03:04.000
can reject a prompt outright or add context alongside it. And because stop runs when Claude

31
00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:09.440
tries to finish, a blocking hook there can prevent the agent from stopping and push it to keep

32
00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:15.919
working. So the turn layer is where you can inspect what goes in and control when the agent is allowed

33
00:03:15.919 --> 00:03:22.320
to be done. Now the innermost pair, and this is the contrast I most want you to remember. Pre-tool

34
00:03:22.320 --> 00:03:29.199
use and post-tool use sound like a matched set, but they behave very differently. Pre-tool use

35
00:03:29.199 --> 00:03:35.279
fires before a tool runs, and it can block the call. That makes it your gate. If you want to guard

36
00:03:35.279 --> 00:03:42.320
against a risky command or stop an unwanted tool entirely, this is the hook. And it blocks by exiting

37
00:03:42.320 --> 00:03:49.759
with code 2. Post-tool use fires after a tool succeeds, and here is the crucial part. It can't

38
00:03:49.759 --> 00:03:55.759
block because the tool has already run. There is nothing left to stop, so you use post-tool use to

39
00:03:55.759 --> 00:04:02.720
react to what happened. Log the result, check the output, or trigger a follow-up. Pre-tool guards.

40
00:04:02.720 --> 00:04:10.479
Post-tool reacts. Don't mix them up. Beyond the regular cadences, a few events fire at their own

41
00:04:10.479 --> 00:04:17.200
specific moments. Pre-compact fires just before context compaction, when Claude code is about to

42
00:04:17.200 --> 00:04:23.200
summarize older context to save room. It can block, so you can protect something from being

43
00:04:23.200 --> 00:04:30.000
summarized away. Notification fires when Claude code sends a notification, and it only observes,

44
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:36.000
so it is purely for alerting you, maybe pinging your phone or terminal. And permission request

45
00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:41.359
fires when a tool call needs a permission decision. It can deny that request, but it is the

46
00:04:41.359 --> 00:04:47.920
exception to the exit code rule. It denies by returning a decision object, not by exiting 2.

47
00:04:48.480 --> 00:04:54.480
So even outside the tidy session, turn, and tool buckets, the same question always applies.

48
00:04:54.480 --> 00:05:00.640
Does this moment let me block or only watch? Let's make the blocking mechanism concrete.

49
00:05:00.640 --> 00:05:07.519
Because it comes down to the exit code, your hook returns. Exit 0 means success. Claude code reads

50
00:05:07.519 --> 00:05:13.519
any structured output your hook printed and just continues normally. Exit 2 is the special one.

51
00:05:13.519 --> 00:05:19.760
It is the blocking signal. For any hook that is allowed to block, exiting 2 stops the action and

52
00:05:19.760 --> 00:05:25.920
feeds your message back to Claude as the reason. And any other exit code is treated as a non-blocking

53
00:05:25.920 --> 00:05:32.880
error for most events, meaning the action still goes ahead anyway. That last point trips people up.

54
00:05:32.880 --> 00:05:38.880
A plain failure code of 1 doesn't block. If you want to enforce a rule, you have to exit 2

55
00:05:38.880 --> 00:05:46.399
specifically.0 to allow, 2 to block, everything else just warns. Let's pull the single most useful

56
00:05:46.399 --> 00:05:52.799
distinction to the front. For any event, the real question is whether the hook can stop an action

57
00:05:52.799 --> 00:05:58.480
or can only watch it. On the blocking side, pre-tool use stops a tool before it runs,

58
00:05:59.119 --> 00:06:04.720
stop keeps the agent working instead of finishing, and pre-compact can halt compaction

59
00:06:04.720 --> 00:06:12.079
before context gets trimmed. On the observe-only side, post-tool use can react but can't undo

60
00:06:12.079 --> 00:06:18.399
because the tool already finished. And session start and notification simply watch and report.

61
00:06:18.399 --> 00:06:24.640
So the practical rule is short. When you need a gate, something that can say no pick a blocking

62
00:06:24.640 --> 00:06:30.799
event. When you just need a record or an alert, an observe-only event is exactly right and it

63
00:06:30.799 --> 00:06:36.480
keeps your hook out of the critical path. Let's put it all together as a decision.

64
00:06:36.480 --> 00:06:41.600
To choose the right hook, think about what you want to happen and when. Do you need to guard a

65
00:06:41.600 --> 00:06:48.320
dangerous command before it runs? Reach for pre-tool use since it can block. Want to auto-format or

66
00:06:48.320 --> 00:06:55.359
lint right after an edit? Use post-tool use, which reacts after the fact. Need to load project

67
00:06:55.359 --> 00:07:01.760
context the moment a session opens? Session start is your hook. The pattern is always the same.

68
00:07:01.760 --> 00:07:08.480
The cadence, session versus turn versus tool, tells you when the hook fires. And the blocking column

69
00:07:08.480 --> 00:07:14.480
tells you whether you can actually intervene or only observe. Answer both of those and the right

70
00:07:14.480 --> 00:07:20.320
hook picks itself. There are a couple of questions tangled together here and they aren't the same

71
00:07:20.320 --> 00:07:26.320
question. When a hook fires is about cadence. Whether it can stop anything is about power.

72
00:07:26.320 --> 00:07:32.480
A hook can sit right in front of a tool call and still be there only to watch. Decide the

73
00:07:32.480 --> 00:07:37.519
moment first, then check whether the moment gives you the veto or just a seat to observe.

74
00:07:38.079 --> 00:07:41.119
That's where we'll leave this lesson. I'll see you in the next one.

